item.text:
Accepted new evidence for Chapter 6B, 2022, no score.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Russian Federation","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/rus/","claim":"In 2022 Russia recorded 97% basic drinking water coverage, 89% basic sanitation coverage, 76% safely managed drinking water coverage, 61% safely managed sanitation coverage, and 0% open defecation.","locator":"line 5049","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF’s country dashboard publishes explicit indicator values for the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target year 2022 fits exactly.","ruler_attribution":"Country-year welfare baseline; not uniquely attributable to Putin, but relevant to the ruler-period record.","contrary_evidence":["UNICEF flags very high effort still needed to meet 2030 targets, so service coverage remains imperfect."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Russian Federation","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/rus/","claim":"Russia’s child-health and schooling indicators were strong by 2022: DTP3 and MCV1 were both 97%, under-five mortality was 5 per 1,000 live births, neonatal mortality was 2, skilled birth attendance was 100% in 2020, lower-secondary completion was 100% in 2020, and upper-secondary completion was 91% in 2020.","locator":"line 4943","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF’s page provides explicit values drawn from internationally comparable data series.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mostly 2022, with clearly labeled adjacent-year measures where shown on the page.","ruler_attribution":"Baseline and outcome context; attribution to Putin is indirect and should be paired with policy evidence.","contrary_evidence":["The same page marks several education and health targets as still requiring effort, so the picture is not uniformly strong."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Russian Federation","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/rus/","claim":"Russia reported 0% children in extreme poverty in 2022, 12% of children below the national poverty line in 2020, and 100% child coverage by social protection floors/systems in 2020.","locator":"lines 5052 and 5063","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF’s dashboard summarizes comparable child-poverty and social-protection indicators with explicit values and years.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mostly 2022, with adjacent-year child-poverty and social-protection indicators explicitly labeled on the page.","ruler_attribution":"Baseline/outcome context; social protection coverage may reflect inherited systems as well as 2022 policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["The dashboard also shows that child-poverty and social-protection data are incomplete in some adjacent dimensions, so this should not be over-read as comprehensive welfare success."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation health system information","publisher":"WHO European Observatory on Health Systems and Policies","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://eurohealthobservatory.who.int/countries/russian-federation","claim":"The Russian Federation has universal health coverage with free care in state and municipal facilities, but quality and availability vary widely by region; prescriptions usually require patient payment except for some protected groups, and since 2019 reforms have focused on improving primary care access, especially in rural areas.","locator":"lines 146-152","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The Observatory is a specialist WHO-affiliated health-system reference and the claims are explicit on the page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"System description is stable context for 2022, not a one-off event.","ruler_attribution":"System-level context rather than direct ruler attribution; useful for judging what feasible improvements Putin could have delivered.","contrary_evidence":["The same source says service quality and availability vary widely by region, which limits any claim of uniform access."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Systematic Country Diagnostic for the Russian Federation: Pathways to Inclusive Growth","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/russia/publication/systematic-country-diagnostic-for-the-russian-federation-pathways-to-inclusive-growth","claim":"The World Bank said Russia needed deeper human-capital gains and better public services, because life expectancy, especially for men, was low and regional and socioeconomic health differences were large; it also said social assistance should be focused more on the most vulnerable, and that pensions had a low adequacy problem.","locator":"lines 68-74","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The diagnostic is a World Bank synthesis of policy-relevant evidence and states the conclusions directly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 baseline context, useful for judging 2022 ruler-period change and feasible policy space.","ruler_attribution":"Context and constraint evidence, not direct proof of 2022 action.","contrary_evidence":["This is a diagnostic, so it identifies problems and policy needs rather than demonstrating that Putin corrected them in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's Economic Recovery Gathers Pace, Says New World Bank Report","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2021-05-26","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2021/05/26/russia-s-economic-recovery-gathers-pace-says-new-world-bank-report","claim":"The World Bank said Russia spent over 3% of GDP, or about $30 billion per year, on social-assistance programs, and that this spending was more than three times the combined income deficit of all poor families before transfers.","locator":"lines 148-151","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"A World Bank press release summarizing a country report with explicit quantitative findings.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Pre-2022 baseline, relevant for judging whether 2022 actions built on or merely inherited an expensive safety net.","ruler_attribution":"Baseline and policy-capacity context rather than direct 2022 attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The same report says a targeted program could reduce poverty faster and at lower fiscal cost, implying the existing system was not fully efficient."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19 (Coronavirus) Policy Response to Protecting the Poorest and Most Vulnerable in Russia","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/russia/brief/covid-19-response-poor-vulnerable-russia","claim":"The World Bank warned that families with children could see poverty rise by 2 or more percentage points, that central and north-western federal districts could see poverty increases twice as large as other regions, and that expanded child allowances and social-assistance measures could partly offset the shock.","locator":"lines 75-80","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The page gives explicit scenario estimates and identifies the mitigation policies.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 crisis policy context that carried into the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Policy-response context; the measures were part of the inherited and ongoing welfare architecture rather than uniquely a 2022 Putin innovation.","contrary_evidence":["The same source says poorer households, children, the elderly, and people with disabilities were especially exposed, showing substantial residual vulnerability."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19 (Coronavirus) Policy Response to Addressing Learning Gaps and Inequalities in Russia","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/russia/brief/covid-19-response-learning-gaps-inequalities-russia","claim":"The World Bank said most children, and especially poor children, could lose up to six months of learning from school disruptions, with under-represented students more likely to drop out or finish with lower qualifications and with widened inequity in learning outcomes.","locator":"lines 60-64","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The World Bank page is explicit about the education shock and distributional effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pandemic-era background that shaped 2022 human-development outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than direct 2022 Putin attribution; useful because the government’s response had to operate under these conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The page notes some distance learning existed, so the loss was not total or uniform."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Skills Development Key to Further Improving Students’ Learning Outcomes, Says World Bank Report","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2021-07-29","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2021/07/29/new-skills-development-key-to-further-improving-students-learning-outcomes-says-world-bank-report","claim":"The World Bank reported that 38% of Russian schools lacked workshops, 46% lacked scientific laboratories, and 77% lacked dedicated spaces for integrated lessons that support teamwork and skill development.","locator":"lines 146-147","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The press release gives direct numeric findings from the underlying report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 system-quality context relevant to 2022 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Context for inherited constraints; not direct evidence of 2022 Putin action.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says Russian students are often strong academically, so the issue is quality and modern skills, not universal failure."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation - Demographic and socioeconomic outcome data in UNICEF country profile","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/rus/","claim":"Russia’s 2022 child welfare profile still showed major service needs despite strong averages, including very high effort required to hit water and sanitation targets and only partial child-poverty progress in the 2020-2022 data series.","locator":"line 5049 and line 5052","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Same UNICEF country dashboard; this claim synthesizes the table’s explicit target-effort labels rather than drawing a new inference.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 target-year fit is explicit on the page.","ruler_attribution":"Context, not direct attribution; helpful for interpreting the baseline-adjusted welfare record.","contrary_evidence":["The same page marks some targets as already met, so the record is mixed rather than uniformly poor."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://data.who.int/countries/643","claim":"WHO’s country profile classifies Russia as high income, shows current health expenditure at 7.39% of GDP in 2021, and tracks under-five mortality for 2022 alongside life-expectancy indicators.","locator":"lines 27-30 and 181-185","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO country data page with explicit country classification and tracked indicators; the page is stronger on baseline context than on ruler attribution.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The page mixes 2021 and 2022 indicators but is clearly in the target-period neighborhood.","ruler_attribution":"Baseline context only.","contrary_evidence":["The page does not itself show Putin-specific policy choices, so it should not be over-attributed."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin orders benefit payments for people arriving in Russia from Ukraine","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-27","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/putin-orders-benefit-payments-for-people-arriving-in-russia-from-ukraine-2881261","claim":"Putin signed a decree introducing financial benefits for people who left Ukrainian territory for Russia, including monthly pension payments of 10,000 roubles for people forced to leave Ukraine, disability support, and a one-off payment for pregnant women.","locator":"lines 419-420","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is reliable reporting and the page states the decree’s terms explicitly, though it is a syndicated host page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Putin action, but the policy was tied to people displaced from Ukraine rather than the general Russian population.","contrary_evidence":["The same report links the benefit policy to Russia-backed territorial claims in Ukraine, so the measure may also have a strategic or political purpose."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's 2022 economic fall seen in lower incomes, slumping consumer demand","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2023-02-08","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russias-2022-economic-fall-seen-in-lower-incomes-slumping-consumer-demand-2998520","claim":"Rosstat data reported by Reuters showed real disposable incomes fell 1% in 2022, retail sales slumped 6.7%, and unemployment stayed at a record low 3.7% in December.","locator":"lines 411-414","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is reliable and the article reports specific Rosstat figures on household income and demand.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context for the Putin period; not a pure policy attribution by itself.","contrary_evidence":["The same article says unemployment remained very low, so the welfare picture is mixed rather than uniformly deteriorating."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian unemployment rate hits record low, economic data paints mixed picture","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russian-unemployment-rate-hits-record-low-economic-data-paints-mixed-picture-2901110","claim":"Reuters reported that Rosstat said real wages in Russia fell 3.2% year on year in July and that car production shrank 69% in annual terms, showing significant sectoral strain even with low unemployment.","locator":"lines 431-439","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is reliable and the article reports specific official data points with concrete figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context for the Putin period; not full causal attribution by itself.","contrary_evidence":["The same article says unemployment was still at a record low, so the economy was not uniformly in collapse."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia says budget deficit hit 2.3% of GDP in 2022","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2023-01-10","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/russia-says-budget-deficit-hit-23-of-gdp-in-2022-2977210","claim":"Russia recorded a 2022 budget deficit of 2.3% of GDP and had to draw on its rainy-day fund while diverting more funds toward security and defence.","locator":"lines 418-423","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reports official fiscal data and the fiscal tradeoff is explicit on the page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 fiscal outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Useful for judging 2022 fiscal capacity and tradeoffs under Putin.","contrary_evidence":["A deficit is not automatically mismanagement; the same report says it reflected war-related spending pressures."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin says Russia will post budget surplus this year, GDP to decline around 2%","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/09/08/russia-economy-putin-forum","claim":"Putin said budget spending was up 20% on the previous year, predicted only about a 2% GDP decline, and acknowledged that economic hardships remained for many Russians.","locator":"lines 603-613","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-transmitted reporting on a Putin speech, with explicit numerical claims and a clear acknowledgment of hardship.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Putin rhetoric and self-assessment, though it remains self-serving official speech evidence.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-reporting by Putin, so it should be weighed against independent outcome evidence rather than taken at face value."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Economic Outlook Report 10/11/2022","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2022-10-11","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/weo/issues/2022/10/11/world-economic-outlook-october-2022","claim":"The IMF said Russia’s invasion of Ukraine was part of the broader cost-of-living crisis weighing on the outlook and that fiscal policy should help alleviate cost-of-living pressures while keeping policy sufficiently tight.","locator":"lines 260-263 and 293-295","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The IMF report directly states the macro policy guidance and the role of the war in the cost-of-living crisis.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 global and Russia-relevant macro context.","ruler_attribution":"Context rather than direct Putin attribution; relevant to the shock environment he had to manage.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF’s advice is generic across countries, so it does not by itself prove good or bad Russian implementation."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human Development Report 2021-22","publisher":"UNDP","publication_date":"2022-09-08","url":"https://www.undp.org/eurasia/publications/HDR-2021-22","claim":"UNDP said the ongoing pandemic had reversed human development in almost every country and that war in Ukraine and elsewhere had created more human suffering and a worldwide cost-of-living crisis.","locator":"lines 41-45","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNDP’s report states the macro-human-development context directly and dates it clearly.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year context.","ruler_attribution":"Context only, not a direct Putin-policy finding.","contrary_evidence":["The report is global and not Russia-specific, so it should be used only as background, not as ruler-level proof."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Education Finance Watch","publisher":"UNESCO / World Bank / UIS","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.unesco.org/gem-report/en/publication/2022-education-finance-watch","claim":"The report says the Covid-19 pandemic exacerbated the global learning crisis and that the 2022 edition analyzes education financing across 2020, 2021, and 2022 using the latest data available as of May 2022.","locator":"lines 397-398","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint UNESCO/World Bank/UIS publication with explicit summary language, but it is global rather than Russia-specific.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 education-policy context.","ruler_attribution":"Context only.","contrary_evidence":["Because it is global, it should not be over-read as direct evidence about Russia’s government choices."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Reimbursable Advisory Services in Russia","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/russia/brief/ras","claim":"The World Bank’s Russia advisory platform describes its health work as expanding access to affordable health care, and its social-protection work as covering safety nets, pensions, labor programs, and delivery systems such as targeting, payments, governance, monitoring, and evaluation.","locator":"lines 170-180","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"The page is a World Bank description of its Russia-facing advisory portfolio, useful for policy context and implementation design.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Still relevant to the 2022 institutional environment.","ruler_attribution":"Context only; it shows what policy tools existed, not that Putin used them well.","contrary_evidence":["This is an advisory-program page, so it is weaker on actual 2022 delivery outcomes than on policy capacity."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation","publisher":"WHO Data","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://data.who.int/countries/643","claim":"WHO’s country page classifies Russia as high income, reports current health expenditure at 7.39% of GDP in 2021, and tracks under-five mortality for 2022 along with life-expectancy indicators.","locator":"lines 27-30 and 181-185","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO country data are authoritative baseline indicators, though this page is more descriptive than policy-attributive.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Mixed 2021-2022 indicator neighborhood, useful as baseline context.","ruler_attribution":"Baseline context only, not direct Putin attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The page does not itself identify ruler-specific policy choices or service-quality changes in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.10"]}

Search ledger:
- Candidates found: about 50
- Documents opened: 32
- Duplicates or mirrors removed: 12
- Irrelevant or out-of-period items: 8
- Inaccessible items: 5
- Accepted distinct URLs: 17
- Distinct domains represented in accepted evidence: 9

Material gaps and next directions:
- I still lack direct 2022 Russian primary documents with clean browser access for budget execution, regional social spending, health-service quality, and school/welfare delivery.
- The strongest missing piece is subnational distribution: who actually benefited in poor regions, minority regions, and large families versus regime-linked groups.
- I also want better Russia-specific 2022 evidence on life expectancy, excess mortality, vaccination catch-up, and child welfare beyond the national averages.
- Best next queries:
  1. Russian-language searches for `единое пособие 2022`, `прожиточный минимум 2022`, `медианная заработная плата 2022`, `уровень бедности 2022`, `ожидаемая продолжительность жизни 2022`, `охват вакцинацией 2022`.
  2. Direct Rosstat, Social Fund, Health Ministry, and Education Ministry releases on 2022 outcomes.
  3. Subnational evidence on regional inequality, school quality, and healthcare access in poorer federal districts.
  4. If you want saturation toward the target of 20-35 URLs from 10 domains, the next best additions are direct government PDFs and Russian statistical releases once browser access cooperates.
